what REDS %810, this seat's own row, booked by me on 20260917.051352 and its agent-doable half landed by me last lap. The pin is deadlocked at 65,534 of 65,536 with 0 of 22 rows foldable, so no ship on this fleet can book a red -- on a fleet whose first law is reds first. reds_pin_capacity_scan.sh names the first door and it is the fleet's own: prove one repair on metal and accrete that row's last bold marker to BOOKED, which moves pin_foldable_rows off zero. %810 fits door B's seated definition exactly. Its defect was that a refusal no roster hears looks exactly like a guard that passes: grep -c fund_prep construction/standing-equipment.kyri read 0, so all seven tools/gen/fund/gen_*_fund_prep.rish witnesses stood on no roster and gen_trya_fund_prep had refused on committed bytes for fifty days unheard. That half stands repaired and re-proven on metal this lap -- six seated at tier lap and GREEN in 113-201 ms apiece, the seventh held out by name with the exact roster lines that seat it written into the roster's own comment. The remainder is a SEAT, which is door B's own third clause: the two doors, retire the generator or rename it forward to gen_acme_fund_prep checking Acme (fund), acme.fund and %acme, are a POLE fund-seat naming ruling on Keaton's word. So the row is BOOKED rather than OPEN, and its marker accretes rather than being rewritten. Then fold it with reds_fold.sh to its own shelf, which frees the pin and lets the fleet book reds again. Patchouli is taking the same deadlock door on %801 and either landing frees the pin; mine differs in that %810's remainder is a named seat with its seating condition already written, where %801's is a fleet question. No fund fact, seat, number, gate or guard verdict moves, and no peer file is touched.
claim diffuser-loom-cross-sitting
seat diffuser
stamp 20260917.072801
epoch 1789644481
paths tools/fixtures/l/loom_sitting_scan.sh tools/l/loom_sitting_witness.rish tools/fixtures/l/loom_sitting_control.sh tools/fixtures/l/loom_values.sh tools/l/loom_trend.sh
what My own last lap's standing card question, and the one the fleet's whole timing habit rests on: the cross-sitting reading has no instrument. tools/l/loom_trend.sh reads a loom key back across 1,577 logs and answers first, last, min, max, mean and direction -- and direction is a single strand doing two jobs, since it cannot tell a real movement from the pier's own load. My 20260917.054941 paper measured the size of that: a wall-clock median moved 306 parts per thousand across six sittings of identical work while its CPU median moved 7, so a trend printing direction=rising off a wall key is very often reading the pier rather than the work. The new instrument answers the question direction cannot: given a loom key, group its values by SITTING (the day shelf the log was born on), take each sitting's own median and spread, and report the WITHIN-sitting spread beside the ACROSS-sitting spread, both in parts per thousand of the median. What a reader gets is a resolution floor -- the smallest change this key could prove on this pier -- and a verdict naming which of three states the key is in: comparable, dominated (within swallows across, so no claim of movement in this key is readable), or insufficient. The single-value sitting is the trap and it is named rather than averaged: a sitting holding one value has spread zero, which is unmeasured rather than noise-free, so those are counted apart and excluded from the within reading, and a key with no multi-value sitting REFUSES instead of printing a confident zero. That is my own last lap's red -- one condition wearing two names -- caught before it is written. The value reading itself moves into a sourced sibling rather than being spelled a second time, and the extraction is proven by the trend's own output standing byte-identical before and after. No loom line, log, claim or peer file is touched; the reading is reported and gates nothing, since whether a key is comparable is a fact about the pier rather than a fault of any lane.
